    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930500/assets/9e36e33a8d00ff486762e0eafad6e1420fab27f8721c7bdd1d1d6c6062cd1332.png)\n\nDave unfolds a cardboard box and gets the net shown above. In the figure, the areas of two of its faces are shown. What is the total surface area of the box?",
    "worked_solution": "The net shown corresponds to a rectangular prism. The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ squares (base and top) and $4$ rectangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =2\\times 4+4\\times 6 \\\\  & =8+24 \\\\  & =32\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
